{
    "background_story": "In ancient mythology, Eliel was a ferryman tasked with guiding souls across the treacherous River Styx. According to legend, the only way to ensure a safe passage was to pay Eliel an obolus, a small coin placed in the mouth of the deceased. Those who failed to pay the toll were doomed to wander the river's banks for eternity. Eliel took his job very seriously, ensuring that every soul was accounted for and that the obolus was paid in full. One day, a poor soul named Theodoros was unable to pay the toll, but Eliel, moved by his story, decided to waive the fee. As Theodoros reached the afterlife, he was met with a hero's welcome, and Eliel's kindness was rewarded with a place among the gods. From then on, the obolus was seen not just as a payment, but as a symbol of Eliel's compassion and fairness.",
